const FIXCOURSE = {"lectures":[{"id":"L04","deck":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf","label":"Day 4 (9/28): Drug–receptor interactions I","prof":"Gottlieb","exam":1,"module":1},{"id":"L10","deck":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf","label":"Day 10 (10/6): Adrenergic pharmacology, indirect-acting drugs and α receptors","prof":"Gottlieb","exam":2,"module":2}],"skills":[{"id":"recall","label":"Recall a fact or definition","short":"Recall"},{"id":"tell","label":"Tell two look-alikes apart","short":"Tell apart"},{"id":"apply","label":"Apply to a scenario","short":"Apply"},{"id":"figure","label":"Read a curve or figure","short":"Curves"},{"id":"calc","label":"Calculate","short":"Calculate"},{"id":"drug","label":"Drug list: mechanism","short":"Drug list"},{"id":"term","label":"Define a term","short":"Terms"}],"topics":[{"id":"L04","name":"Day 4: inverse agonists, allosterics, antagonists","prof":"Gottlieb","lecture":"L04","cite":"Day 4 (9/28) — Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf (Part 1)","subs":[{"id":"recap","name":"Two-state recap: full and partial agonists","cite":"Day 3 deck slides ~31–~65 (lectured 9/28); Day 4 deck slide ~3"},{"id":"ia","name":"Inverse agonists","cite":"slides ~4–~24"},{"id":"allo","name":"Allosteric agonists and antagonists","cite":"slides ~25–~34, ~36–~42"},{"id":"antag","name":"Kinds of antagonism","cite":"slides ~35–~37"},{"id":"comp","name":"Competitive antagonist","cite":"slides ~43–~50, ~55"},{"id":"irrev","name":"Irreversible antagonist and spare receptors","cite":"slides ~51–~56"}]},{"id":"L10","name":"Day 10: adrenergic receptors, indirect-acting drugs, α1 and α2","prof":"Gottlieb","lecture":"L10","cite":"Day 10 (10/6) — PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf (slides 1–32 taught)","subs":[{"id":"rec","name":"Adrenergic receptors and their G proteins","cite":"slides 2–3"},{"id":"map","name":"Heart, circulation and the MAP equation","cite":"slides 4–7"},{"id":"ne","name":"Norepinephrine synthesis, release and removal","cite":"slide 8"},{"id":"class","name":"Direct, mixed and indirect agonists","cite":"slide 9"},{"id":"indirect","name":"Cocaine, amphetamines and methylphenidate","cite":"slides 10–12"},{"id":"mao","name":"MAO inhibitors and the cheese effect","cite":"slides 13–15"},{"id":"a1","name":"α1 receptors: phenylephrine, oxymetazoline","cite":"slides 16–19"},{"id":"a2","name":"α2 receptors: clonidine","cite":"slides 20–24, 26"},{"id":"a1ant","name":"α antagonists: prazosin and the -osins","cite":"slides 27–30"},{"id":"mirt","name":"Mirtazapine (α2 antagonist)","cite":"slides 31–32"}]}],"qs":[{"id":"L04-009","lecture":"L04","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L04","sub":"recap","skill":"recall","concept":"partial-agonist-table","tags":["poll","albuterol"],"source":"both","stem":"Which of the following drugs is a partial agonist?","options":[{"t":"Albuterol","correct":true,"why":"Albuterol is the β2 partial agonist on the drug list; it opens the airways enough for an asthma patient to breathe without opening them 100%."},{"t":"Epinephrine","correct":false,"why":"Epinephrine is a full agonist."},{"t":"Histamine","correct":false,"why":"Histamine is the body’s full agonist at histamine receptors."},{"t":"Acetylcholine","correct":false,"why":"Acetylcholine is a full agonist."},{"t":"Metoprolol","correct":false,"why":"Metoprolol is a competitive antagonist at β1."}],"teach":"From the drug list, albuterol is the partial agonist; the others in this poll are agonists (epinephrine, histamine, acetylcholine) or an antagonist (metoprolol). A partial agonist is used when the maximal response is not needed: albuterol opens the airways just enough for an asthma patient to breathe better.","quote":"If your patient has asthma, albuterol, which is a partial agonist, is going to open up your airways just enough for you to breathe better in and out. You don’t have to open your lungs 100%.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slide ~3; transcript 9/28"},{"id":"L04-013","lecture":"L04","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L04","sub":"ia","skill":"recall","concept":"fa-plus-inverse-emax-why","tags":["histamine","loratadine"],"source":"both","stem":"In the presence of loratadine, histamine still reaches its maximal response if enough histamine is released. Which property of loratadine makes this possible?","options":[{"t":"It is reversible","correct":true,"why":"Loratadine binds and comes off, so whichever drug has the higher concentration wins the receptors; enough histamine outcompetes it."},{"t":"It has positive efficacy","correct":false,"why":"Loratadine has negative efficacy; efficacy is not what lets histamine reach Emax."},{"t":"It binds an allosteric site","correct":false,"why":"Loratadine competes for the same orthosteric pocket as histamine."},{"t":"It forms covalent bonds","correct":false,"why":"Covalent binding would make it irreversible and would lower the Emax instead."}],"teach":"Any drug that is reversible, competitive and surmountable can be outcompeted: the agonist only has to be present at a higher concentration than the inverse agonist. That is why the inverse agonist changes potency (shift right) and baseline but never the ability of the full agonist to reach Emax. A higher loratadine dose simply requires more histamine to outcompete it.","quote":"They are reversible. As long as I can outcompete them because they’re going to bind and they’re going to come off, whoever has the highest concentration is going to occupy the most receptors to it.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~14, ~21; transcript 9/28"},{"id":"L04-015","lecture":"L04","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L04","sub":"ia","skill":"recall","concept":"constitutive-activity-inverse","tags":["loratadine"],"source":"both","stem":"Which receptors are more sensitive to inverse agonists?","options":[{"t":"Receptors with constitutive activity","correct":true,"why":"Receptors that are active on their own give the inverse agonist something to shut down; antihistamines such as loratadine and cetirizine act this way."},{"t":"Receptors held entirely in the inactive state","correct":false,"why":"With every receptor inactive an inverse agonist produces a flat line at 0%."},{"t":"Receptors bound by an irreversible antagonist","correct":false,"why":"Covalently blocked receptors are not available to any other drug."},{"t":"Receptors with no orthosteric site","correct":false,"why":"Inverse agonists in this course are orthosteric and compete for the binding pocket."}],"teach":"Receptors that have constitutive activity (active by themselves, without any drug, hormone or neurotransmitter) are more sensitive to inverse agonists. The antihistamines cetirizine and loratadine are the classic inverse agonists; histamine, opioid, cannabinoid, dopamine, bradykinin and adenosine receptors are listed as showing this. Loratadine is the one inverse agonist to know on the drug list.","quote":"Receptors that have constitutive activity are more sensitive to inverse agonists.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slide ~23; transcript 9/28"},{"id":"L04-017","lecture":"L04","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L04","sub":"allo","skill":"recall","concept":"allosteric-two-things","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"What are the two things to know about an allosteric agonist?","options":[{"t":"Two drugs bound at once; affects affinity or efficacy or both","correct":true,"why":"It binds a different site, so the agonist can be bound at the same time, and it changes the pocket in a way that alters affinity, efficacy or both."},{"t":"Competes for the orthosteric pocket; has positive efficacy of its own","correct":false,"why":"An allosteric drug binds someplace else and does not compete for the pocket."},{"t":"Forms covalent bonds; lowers the receptor pool","correct":false,"why":"Covalent, pool-lowering binding is the irreversible antagonist; all allosterics in this course are reversible."},{"t":"Binds only inactive receptors; has negative efficacy","correct":false,"why":"That describes the inverse agonist."}],"teach":"Allosteric drugs are the exception to one-or-the-other binding: two drugs can be bound to the receptor at the same time. The allosteric agonist binds someplace else in the receptor and changes the orthosteric pocket so the agonist binds more easily, increasing its affinity, its efficacy or both. For this course all allosterics are reversible.","quote":"Allosteric: 2 Things you should know - Two drugs bound at the same time - Affinity or Efficacy or Both.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~25–~28; transcript 9/28"},{"id":"L04-021","lecture":"L04","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L04","sub":"antag","skill":"tell","concept":"chemical-vs-physiological-antagonism","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors lowers heart rate while epinephrine at β1 receptors raises it. What kind of antagonism is this?","options":[{"t":"Physiological","correct":true,"why":"Two agonists acting independently at different receptors in the same organ produce opposing effects."},{"t":"Chemical","correct":false,"why":"Chemical antagonism is a direct chemical interaction between the agonist and the antagonist, as with chelating agents such as dimercaprol."},{"t":"Competitive (orthosteric)","correct":false,"why":"The two drugs bind different receptors; they do not compete for the same pocket."},{"t":"Allosteric","correct":false,"why":"Neither drug binds another site on the other’s receptor."}],"teach":"Chemical antagonism: a direct chemical interaction between the agonist and the antagonist, for example chelating agents such as dimercaprol used to treat gold, mercury or arsenic poisoning. Physiological antagonism: two agonists acting independently of each other on different receptors and producing opposing effects, for example acetylcholine and epinephrine in the heart. The pharmacological antagonists (competitive, irreversible, allosteric) are the focus.","quote":"Physiological: Two agonist acting independent of each other producing opposing effects. Ach and Epi in the heart.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slide ~35; transcript 9/28"},{"id":"L04-022","lecture":"L04","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L04","sub":"antag","skill":"recall","concept":"antagonist-synonyms","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which term is another name for a competitive antagonist?","options":[{"t":"Surmountable","correct":true,"why":"Competitive, reversible and surmountable are different names for the same drug: it binds and comes off, and the agonist can outcompete it."},{"t":"Insurmountable","correct":false,"why":"Insurmountable names the irreversible antagonist, which cannot be overcome by more agonist."},{"t":"Non-equilibrium","correct":false,"why":"Non-equilibrium names the irreversible antagonist; there is no balance between binding and unbinding."},{"t":"Non-competitive","correct":false,"why":"Non-competitive covers the irreversible antagonist (binds and does not come off) and the allosteric antagonist (binds someplace else)."}],"teach":"Pharmacological antagonisms: competitive (orthosteric) = surmountable = reversible, the most common in clinical practice, agonist and antagonist competing for the same receptor site; nonequilibrium-competitive (orthosteric) = irreversible = insurmountable, cannot be overcome by increasing the agonist concentration; allosteric antagonist = non-competitive, reduces the agonist’s affinity and/or efficacy from another site.","quote":"One is competitive antagonist, also known as reversible antagonists, also known as surmountable antagonists, different names for the same drug. They bind and they come off.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slide ~36; transcript 9/28"},{"id":"L04-023","lecture":"L04","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L04","sub":"antag","skill":"recall","concept":"pam-nam","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"In the literature, what is a negative allosteric modulator (NAM)?","options":[{"t":"An allosteric antagonist","correct":true,"why":"A NAM binds another site and reduces the agonist’s affinity and/or efficacy."},{"t":"An allosteric agonist","correct":false,"why":"The allosteric agonist is the positive allosteric modulator (PAM)."},{"t":"An inverse agonist","correct":false,"why":"An inverse agonist is orthosteric with negative efficacy, not an allosteric ligand."},{"t":"An irreversible antagonist","correct":false,"why":"The irreversible antagonist binds the orthosteric pocket covalently."}],"teach":"Allosteric ligands: the allosteric agonist is a positive allosteric modulator (PAM); the allosteric antagonist is a negative allosteric modulator (NAM). Whatever the positive one does (raises affinity, efficacy or both), the negative one does the opposite.","quote":"we can also call allosteric agonists PAMs, positive allosteric modulators, you see that in the literature, or NAMs, negative allosteric modulators, but the key thing is if you know what the positive does, the negative is the opposite.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slide ~37; transcript 9/28"},{"id":"L04-025","lecture":"L04","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L04","sub":"allo","skill":"tell","concept":"noncompetitive-two-reasons","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Irreversible antagonists and allosteric antagonists are both called non-competitive. What makes each one non-competitive?","options":[{"t":"Irreversible: binds and does not come off; allosteric: binds a different site","correct":true,"why":"No competition is possible with a drug that never leaves the pocket, or with a drug that never enters it."},{"t":"Both bind the orthosteric pocket covalently","correct":false,"why":"Only the irreversible antagonist forms covalent bonds; allosterics are reversible in this course."},{"t":"Both have negative efficacy","correct":false,"why":"Negative efficacy is the inverse agonist; these antagonists have no pharmacological efficacy of their own."},{"t":"Irreversible: binds a different site; allosteric: binds and does not come off","correct":false,"why":"The reasons are reversed."}],"teach":"Two different reasons give the same label. The irreversible antagonist forms covalent bonds in the orthosteric pocket and never comes off, so there is no equilibrium or competition. The allosteric antagonist binds reversibly at another site in the receptor, so it is not competing for the pocket at all; the agonist cannot overcome it and the shifts it causes are asymmetrical.","quote":"One because one binds and doesn’t come off, the other one because it binds someplace else. There’s no competition with it along the way.","cite":"Pharmacodynamics-Day_4_-_5_2026s_pptx.pdf slides ~36, ~41; transcript 9/28"},{"id":"L10-001","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"rec","skill":"recall","concept":"adrenergic-g-proteins","tags":["alpha"],"source":"both","stem":"Which of the following statements about the adrenergic receptors and their G proteins is CORRECT?","options":[{"t":"α2 is coupled to Gi","correct":true,"why":"α2 is coupled to Gi: inhibition of adenylyl cyclase (AC) and less cyclic AMP (cAMP)."},{"t":"α1 is coupled to Gs","correct":false,"why":"α1 is coupled to Gq (phospholipase C, IP3, Ca++), not Gs."},{"t":"β1 is coupled to Gi","correct":false,"why":"All β receptors are Gs; β1 activates AC and raises cAMP."},{"t":"β2 is coupled to Gq","correct":false,"why":"β2 is Gs (activation of AC, more cAMP)."},{"t":"β3 is coupled to Gi","correct":false,"why":"β3 is also Gs: it increases AC and cAMP."}],"teach":"Adrenergic receptors and their G proteins: α1 → Gq → phospholipase C (PLC) → IP3; α2 → Gi → inhibition of adenylyl cyclase (AC) → less cyclic AMP (cAMP); β1, β2 and β3 → Gs → activation of AC → more cAMP. All the β receptors are Gs, so they act in the same direction on the second messenger.","quote":"We have alpha 1s, alpha 2s, beta 1s, beta 2s, and beta 3s. The good news is all betas are GS, so you kind of know what they're going to do ... alpha 2s are GI, and alpha 1s are GQ.","note":"In the same sentence he also said “alpha 2s are GQ” before correcting himself to “alpha 2s are GI”; the slide table gives α2 = Gi. Keyed to the slide.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 3; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-002","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"rec","skill":"tell","concept":"alpha2-like-m2-alpha1-like-m3","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which adrenergic receptor uses the same G protein and second-messenger change as the muscarinic M2 receptor?","options":[{"t":"α2","correct":true,"why":"M2 and α2 are both Gi: inhibition of adenylyl cyclase and less cAMP."},{"t":"α1","correct":false,"why":"α1 is Gq, like M3 (PLC, IP3, ↑Ca++)."},{"t":"β1","correct":false,"why":"β1 is Gs, which raises cAMP, the opposite of M2."},{"t":"β2","correct":false,"why":"β2 is Gs and raises cAMP."},{"t":"β3","correct":false,"why":"β3 is Gs and raises cAMP."}],"teach":"The adrenergic receptors reuse the muscarinic second-messenger patterns. α2, like M2, is Gi (inhibition of adenylyl cyclase, less cyclic AMP). α1, like M3, is Gq (phospholipase C, IP3, more Ca++). They differ in where in the body they are located.","quote":"So if you know what M2 does, you know what alpha-2 is gonna do. If you know what M3 does, alpha-1 is gonna do a similar process. They're just located in different parts of our body","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 3; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-003","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"rec","skill":"recall","concept":"beta3-bladder","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Where do β3 receptors have their greatest effect?","options":[{"t":"Bladder","correct":true,"why":"β3 (Gs, more cAMP) has its primary location, where it has its greatest effect, in the bladder, which it relaxes."},{"t":"Heart","correct":false,"why":"The heart is run by β1."},{"t":"Resistance arteries","correct":false,"why":"Arteries and veins are run by α1 (with β2 causing vasodilation)."},{"t":"Brain","correct":false,"why":"In the brain the key adrenergic receptor is α2 (Gi), which suppresses sympathetic outflow."},{"t":"Salivary glands","correct":false,"why":"No β3 site was given for the glands."}],"teach":"β3 is coupled to Gs like β1 and β2: it increases adenylyl cyclase (AC) and cyclic AMP (cAMP). Its primary site, where it has the greatest effect, is the bladder, which lets drugs act there with fewer side effects.","quote":"The one thing that you need to remember about beta 3 is their primary location where they have the greatest effect is gonna be in the bladder, OK?","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 3; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-004","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"rec","skill":"recall","multi":true,"concept":"beta-gs-camp-raising","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Activation of which adrenergic receptors increases cyclic AMP (cAMP)? Select all that apply.","options":[{"t":"β1","correct":true,"why":"β1 → Gs → activation of adenylyl cyclase (AC) → more cAMP."},{"t":"β2","correct":true,"why":"β2 → Gs → activation of AC → more cAMP."},{"t":"β3","correct":true,"why":"β3 → Gs → ↑AC → ↑cAMP."},{"t":"α1","correct":false,"why":"α1 → Gq → phospholipase C → IP3 and Ca++; not cAMP."},{"t":"α2","correct":false,"why":"α2 → Gi → inhibition of AC → cAMP goes down."}],"teach":"Only the β receptors raise cAMP: all three are Gs and activate adenylyl cyclase (AC). α2 does the opposite (Gi, less cAMP), and α1 works through Gq, phospholipase C, IP3 and calcium.","quote":"They're gonna increase the GS activity, they're gonna increase AC which is gonna increase camp.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 3; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-005","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"map","skill":"tell","concept":"preload-vs-afterload","tags":["in-class","alpha1"],"source":"both","stem":"Which statement about preload and afterload is CORRECT?","options":[{"t":"Preload is the blood in the ventricle at end diastole","correct":true,"why":"Preload = end-diastolic volume (diastolic pressure): how much blood is in the relaxed ventricle, ready to be pumped."},{"t":"Afterload is the blood returning to the heart","correct":false,"why":"Blood returning to the heart is preload (venous return)."},{"t":"Preload is the pressure needed to open the aortic valve","correct":false,"why":"The pressure the heart must overcome to open the valve is afterload (systolic pressure)."},{"t":"Afterload is set mainly by the veins","correct":false,"why":"Afterload is set by the resistance arteries; the veins set preload."},{"t":"Preload and afterload both mean heart rate","correct":false,"why":"Heart rate and contractility are the factors within the heart; preload and afterload are outside it."}],"teach":"Preload (diastolic pressure, end-diastolic volume) is the blood in the ventricle at the end of diastole, set by venous return. Afterload (systolic pressure) is the force the ventricle must overcome to open the valve and push blood out, set by the resistance arteries. Heart rate and contractility are the factors within the heart.","quote":"So preload is how much blood is in the ventricle at the top of diastole when your ventricle is the most relaxed, right? Another word for that is called end diastolic volume or diastolic pressure. ... So afterload is the same thing as systolic pressure, right? What is the pressure in your resistant arteries?","note":"He asked the class “So what is preload?” and “What about afterload?”; a student answered “The resistance of the vessels” for afterload.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slides 4, 6; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-006","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"map","skill":"apply","concept":"afterload-cardiac-output","tags":["alpha1"],"source":"both","stem":"With nothing else changed, an increase in afterload is most likely to do which of the following?","options":[{"t":"Decrease stroke volume","correct":true,"why":"More of the heart's work goes into opening the valve, leaving less to push blood out: stroke volume and cardiac output fall."},{"t":"Increase stroke volume","correct":false,"why":"A higher afterload means the heart spends more of its work opening the aortic valve, so less blood is ejected per beat; stroke volume rises only when afterload falls (a vasodilator)."},{"t":"Decrease preload directly","correct":false,"why":"A fall in stroke volume from higher afterload is compensated by an increase in preload, not a decrease."},{"t":"Leave cardiac output unchanged","correct":false,"why":"Afterload affects how much the heart can eject, so cardiac output changes."}],"teach":"Afterload is the pressure the ventricle must overcome to open the valve. The higher it is, the more work the heart does just to open the valve and the less blood it squeezes out, so stroke volume and cardiac output fall; this must be compensated by an increase in preload. A vasodilator lowers afterload and frees the heart to eject more.","quote":"The higher the afterload is, the work, the more work my heart has to do to open that valve and squeeze it out. So that's going to decrease my cardiac output. ... At the same token, if we give vasodilators ... now I have to work less to open the valve and I have more energy to squeeze the blood out, thus increasing the cardiac output.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 6; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-007","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","level":1,"topic":"L10","sub":"map","skill":"tell","concept":"cv-receptor-roles","tags":["beta1"],"source":"both","stem":"Which of the following statements about the adrenergic receptors of the cardiovascular system is CORRECT?","options":[{"t":"β1 sets heart rate and contractility","correct":true,"why":"Within the heart, β1 (Gs, more cAMP) controls how fast it beats and how strongly it contracts."},{"t":"α1 in the veins sets afterload","correct":false,"why":"α1 in the veins sets preload (venous return); afterload is α1 in the resistance arteries."},{"t":"α1 in the resistance arteries sets preload","correct":false,"why":"α1 in the resistance arteries sets afterload."},{"t":"α2 in the brain raises sympathetic outflow","correct":false,"why":"α2 is Gi and inhibitory; in the brain it suppresses the sympathetic system."},{"t":"β2 causes vasoconstriction","correct":false,"why":"β2 causes vasodilation, a check on α1 vasoconstriction."}],"teach":"The cardiovascular map: α1 in the veins (preload) and in the resistance arteries (afterload), both directly innervated by the sympathetic system; β1 in the heart (heart rate, contractility, rate of relaxation) and kidney (renin); β2 causes vasodilation to counter constriction; α2 in the brain and on nerves suppresses sympathetic outflow. Placing a receptor on the mean arterial pressure equation predicts what a drug acting there will do.","quote":"So in the heart, within the heart, the beta ones run the show. Those are the receptors that are going to affect how fast your heart is beating and how strongly it is contracting in the arteries. It's going to be our alpha 1 adrenergic receptors.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slides 4–5; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-008","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","level":1,"topic":"L10","sub":"map","skill":"apply","concept":"venous-alpha1-preload","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Sympathetic activity to the α1 receptors in the veins increases. Which change is most likely?","options":[{"t":"Preload rises","correct":true,"why":"Squeezing the veins (Gq, ↑Ca++) sends more blood back to the heart: more venous return, more preload."},{"t":"Preload falls","correct":false,"why":"Relaxed veins pool blood in the feet and lower preload; α1 activation does the opposite."},{"t":"Heart rate falls through β1","correct":false,"why":"Venous α1 does not act on β1; the question is about venous tone."},{"t":"Veins dilate through Gi","correct":false,"why":"α1 is Gq: more calcium and constriction, not Gi."}],"teach":"Venous return depends on venous tone. Norepinephrine released by sympathetic nerves activates α1 (Gq → ↑Ca++ → constriction) in the veins, squeezing blood back to the heart and raising preload. When the veins relax, blood stays in the feet and preload falls.","quote":"So, as I squeeze the veins, depending on how much tone of my sympathetics are activating those alpha-1 in the veins, that's gonna determine how much blood returns to the heart and how much blood comes out of the heart.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 5; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-009","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","level":1,"topic":"L10","sub":"map","skill":"recall","concept":"beta1-kidney-renin","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Activation of β1 receptors in the kidney mainly increases the production of which substance?","options":[{"t":"Renin","correct":true,"why":"Renal β1 increases renin, which leads to more angiotensin II, a vasoconstrictor."},{"t":"Nitric oxide","correct":false,"why":"Nitric oxide comes from endothelial M3 activation, not renal β1."},{"t":"Acetylcholine","correct":false,"why":"Acetylcholine is released by cholinergic nerves, not made by renal β1."},{"t":"Norepinephrine","correct":false,"why":"Norepinephrine is released by sympathetic nerves; β1 is the receptor it acts on."}],"teach":"The kidney is the long-term regulator of blood pressure. β1 receptors there increase renin production, which makes more angiotensin II, a vasoconstrictor that raises blood pressure. Blocking renal β1 therefore lowers renin release.","quote":"And finally, we have a long-term component over here that is involved in the long-term regulation of blood pressure, and that's gonna be the kidneys. OK, and in the kidneys, the beta ones also can affect its function. Primarily by increasing the production of what? Ren, which it makes more gen testing 2, which is a vasoconstrictor, right?","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 5; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-010","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"map","skill":"recall","concept":"map-equation","tags":["exam-cue","alpha1"],"source":"both","stem":"Which equation gives mean arterial pressure (MAP)?","options":[{"t":"MAP = CO × TPR","correct":true,"why":"Mean arterial pressure equals cardiac output (CO) times total peripheral resistance (TPR), and CO = heart rate (HR) × stroke volume (SV)."},{"t":"MAP = CO + TPR","correct":false,"why":"The two terms multiply, not add."},{"t":"MAP = CO ÷ TPR","correct":false,"why":"Raising resistance raises pressure; it does not divide it."},{"t":"MAP = HR × TPR","correct":false,"why":"Heart rate alone is incomplete: cardiac output is HR × stroke volume."},{"t":"MAP = SV × HR","correct":false,"why":"HR × SV is cardiac output, only one of the two terms."}],"teach":"MAP = CO (HR × SV) × TPR: mean arterial pressure equals cardiac output (heart rate × stroke volume) times total peripheral resistance. Drugs lower MAP by lowering cardiac output (less contractility and heart rate, less ventricular filling) or TPR (vasodilation of resistance vessels, less sympathetic tone).","quote":"Some drugs are going to be more selective to one component versus the other, but the goal over here is for us to be able to predict what component of the aminote pressure formula is being affected by our drugs.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 7; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-011","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","level":1,"topic":"L10","sub":"map","skill":"apply","concept":"carvedilol-map","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Carvedilol blocks β1, β2 and α1 receptors. Which describes its effect on the mean arterial pressure (MAP) equation?","options":[{"t":"It lowers both CO and TPR","correct":true,"why":"β1 block decreases the heart's effects (cardiac output, CO); α1 block in arteries and veins decreases total peripheral resistance (TPR)."},{"t":"It lowers CO only","correct":false,"why":"Its α1 block also relaxes arteries and veins, lowering TPR."},{"t":"It lowers TPR only","correct":false,"why":"Its β1 block also lowers the heart's rate and force, lowering CO."},{"t":"It raises CO and lowers TPR","correct":false,"why":"Blocking β1 lowers, not raises, cardiac output."}],"teach":"MAP = cardiac output (CO) × total peripheral resistance (TPR). Carvedilol blocks β1 (heart: less rate and contractility, lower CO) and α1 (arteries and veins: vasodilation, lower TPR), so it acts on both terms. The skill is to place each drug on the part of the equation it changes.","quote":"As such, if I block the beta ones, I can decrease the effects in the heart, and if I block the alpha ones, I can decrease the effects in the arteries and the veins, thus decreasing my total peripheral resistance. So carvedilol can do it all.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 7; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-012","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"map","skill":"apply","concept":"blood-pressure-rules","tags":["alpha1"],"source":"both","stem":"A drug lowers a patient's blood pressure. Which reflex change is most likely?","options":[{"t":"Heart rate goes up","correct":true,"why":"“Blood pressure rules”: the baroreceptors sense the fall and raise sympathetic drive, so heart rate goes up to restore mean arterial pressure."},{"t":"Heart rate goes down","correct":false,"why":"Heart rate falls when blood pressure rises, not when it falls."},{"t":"Heart rate does not change","correct":false,"why":"The body defends mean arterial pressure, so heart rate responds."},{"t":"The veins dilate further","correct":false,"why":"The compensation is more sympathetic drive, which constricts rather than dilates."}],"teach":"Mean arterial pressure is defended: when blood pressure falls, sympathetic drive and heart rate rise; when it rises, heart rate falls. This is why vasodilators such as prazosin cause reflex tachycardia.","quote":"And there's a rule that we need to follow, and the rule is blood blood pressure rules. What do I mean by that? If my blood pressure goes down, heart rate goes up. If my blood pressure goes up, heart rate goes down.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 5; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-013","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","level":1,"topic":"L10","sub":"map","skill":"recall","concept":"beta1-rate-of-relaxation","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Besides heart rate and contractility, which cardiac function does β1 activation increase?","options":[{"t":"Rate of relaxation","correct":true,"why":"β1 (Gs) increases heart rate, contractility and the rate of relaxation, so the ventricle can refill between fast beats."},{"t":"Afterload","correct":false,"why":"Afterload is set by α1 in the resistance arteries."},{"t":"Venous capacitance","correct":false,"why":"Venous tone is α1 in the veins."},{"t":"Acetylcholine release","correct":false,"why":"β1 does not control acetylcholine release."}],"teach":"β1 receptors in the heart are Gs: they increase heart rate, contractility and the rate of relaxation. Relaxation takes energy too; if the heart beats fast but does not relax fast enough, the ventricles cannot fill.","quote":"We're going to increase the heart rate. We're going to increase the contractility, and also we're going to increase the rate of relaxation. ... It's just how fast your heart has to relax to fill the blood so that it can pump again.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 5; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-014","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"ne","skill":"recall","concept":"ne-synthesis","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which sequence shows the synthesis of norepinephrine?","options":[{"t":"Tyrosine → L-Dopa → dopamine → norepinephrine","correct":true,"why":"In the sympathetic nerve terminal tyrosine is made into L-Dopa, L-Dopa into dopamine, and dopamine into norepinephrine, which is stored in vesicles."},{"t":"L-Dopa → tyrosine → dopamine → norepinephrine","correct":false,"why":"Tyrosine comes first; L-Dopa is made from it."},{"t":"Tyrosine → dopamine → L-Dopa → norepinephrine","correct":false,"why":"L-Dopa comes before dopamine."},{"t":"Dopamine → tyrosine → L-Dopa → norepinephrine","correct":false,"why":"Dopamine is made from L-Dopa, which is made from tyrosine."}],"teach":"In the sympathetic nerve terminal, tyrosine → L-Dopa → dopamine → norepinephrine (NE). NE is stored in vesicles and released on an action potential, like acetylcholine in the cholinergic nerve.","quote":"So very similar to the muscarinic, uh, to the acetylcholines, norepinephrine is gonna be made by a precursor, and it's gonna be stored in a vesicle, and upon an extra potential, we're gonna have the release of norepinephrine to the synapse.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 8; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-015","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"ne","skill":"recall","concept":"presynaptic-alpha2-feedback","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"What does activation of the presynaptic α2 receptor do?","options":[{"t":"Suppresses norepinephrine release","correct":true,"why":"Presynaptic α2 (Gi) is negative feedback: activating it suppresses further release of norepinephrine."},{"t":"Increases norepinephrine release","correct":false,"why":"More release is what blocking the α2 autoreceptor gives (mirtazapine): the Gi brake on release is removed. Activating it does the opposite."},{"t":"Breaks down norepinephrine","correct":false,"why":"Breakdown is done by the enzymes COMT and MAO."},{"t":"Recycles norepinephrine into vesicles","correct":false,"why":"Recycling is the norepinephrine transporter (NET)."},{"t":"Contracts the effector cell","correct":false,"why":"The effector cell carries postsynaptic α1 and β receptors, not the presynaptic α2."}],"teach":"Postsynaptically, norepinephrine acts on α1 or β receptors. Presynaptically sits α2, coupled to Gi: it is negative feedback that suppresses further norepinephrine release.","quote":"Typically, on the post-synaptic side, we're gonna have either alpha one or beta ones, OK? On the pre-synaptic side, we're typically gonna have alpha-2s. Which are negative feedback. This is what's going to suppress the release of norepinephrine. This is our gatekeeper.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 8; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-016","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"ne","skill":"recall","multi":true,"concept":"ne-removal","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which remove released norepinephrine from the synapse? Select all that apply.","options":[{"t":"Catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT)","correct":true,"why":"One of the two enzymes that break norepinephrine down to inactive metabolites."},{"t":"Monoamine oxidase (MAO)","correct":true,"why":"The other enzyme that breaks norepinephrine down."},{"t":"Norepinephrine transporter (NET)","correct":true,"why":"The NET takes norepinephrine back so it can be recycled into the vesicle."},{"t":"Acetylcholinesterase","correct":false,"why":"Acetylcholinesterase breaks down acetylcholine, not norepinephrine."},{"t":"Presynaptic α2 receptor","correct":false,"why":"α2 lowers release; it does not remove norepinephrine already released."}],"teach":"After release, norepinephrine is removed by two enzymes, catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT) and monoamine oxidase (MAO), and, more economically, by the norepinephrine transporter (NET), which recycles it back into the vesicle. Each is a drug target: NET blockers and MAO inhibitors raise norepinephrine.","quote":"We have two enzymes, the COAMT or the MAO, right? And they're gonna cut it down, break down the norepinephrine. More economically, can we move them to the net transporter, the norepinephrine transporter, and recycle it back into the vesicle so that we don't have to make new ones.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 8; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-017","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"ne","skill":"tell","concept":"net-recycles","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which of the following returns norepinephrine to the nerve so it can be stored in the vesicle again?","options":[{"t":"Norepinephrine transporter (NET)","correct":true,"why":"The NET moves norepinephrine back into the nerve to be recycled into the vesicle."},{"t":"Monoamine oxidase (MAO)","correct":false,"why":"MAO breaks norepinephrine down; it does not recycle it."},{"t":"Catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT)","correct":false,"why":"COMT breaks norepinephrine down to inactive metabolites."},{"t":"Presynaptic α2 receptor","correct":false,"why":"α2 suppresses release; it does not take norepinephrine up."}],"teach":"Removal routes for norepinephrine: two enzymes that destroy it (COMT, MAO) and one transporter that recycles it (NET). Cocaine blocks the NET; phenelzine and selegiline block MAO.","quote":"If we have too much of it, the enzymes come and break it. If we don't have enough, we might shunt more through the transporter pathways, right?","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 8; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-018","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"class","skill":"tell","concept":"oxymetazoline-nonselective","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which drug is a non-selective, direct-acting α1 and α2 agonist?","options":[{"t":"Oxymetazoline","correct":true,"why":"Oxymetazoline binds and activates both α1 and α2, so it is non-selective."},{"t":"Phenylephrine","correct":false,"why":"Phenylephrine is a selective α1 agonist."},{"t":"Clonidine","correct":false,"why":"Clonidine is a selective α2 agonist."},{"t":"Cocaine","correct":false,"why":"Cocaine is indirect acting (reuptake inhibitor)."},{"t":"Selegiline","correct":false,"why":"Selegiline is indirect acting (MAO inhibitor)."}],"teach":"A selective drug binds one receptor; a drug that binds two or more is non-selective. Selective direct agonists: phenylephrine (α1), clonidine (α2). Non-selective direct agonists: oxymetazoline (α1 and α2), and epinephrine and norepinephrine (α1, α2, β1, β2).","quote":"And some of those drugs are gonna be selective, they only bind to one receptor or non-selective. They bind to one or more receptors. If you bind to two or more, you're not selective, OK?","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slides 9, 19; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-019","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"class","skill":"recall","concept":"ephedrine-mixed","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Ephedrine is classified as mixed acting. Which describes it?","options":[{"t":"Activates α and β receptors and releases norepinephrine","correct":true,"why":"Ephedrine is an α1, α2, β1, β2 agonist and a releasing agent: direct and indirect at once."},{"t":"Blocks the norepinephrine transporter only","correct":false,"why":"Blocking the norepinephrine transporter (NET) alone is cocaine, an indirect drug that never touches the receptor; ephedrine binds the receptors itself and also pushes norepinephrine out."},{"t":"Irreversibly blocks MAO-A and MAO-B (monoamine oxidase)","correct":false,"why":"Irreversible MAO block is phenelzine, an indirect drug that stops breakdown; ephedrine neither binds the enzyme nor acts only indirectly."},{"t":"Selectively activates α1 receptors","correct":false,"why":"Selective α1 activation is phenylephrine, a purely direct agonist; ephedrine activates α and β receptors and releases norepinephrine as well."}],"teach":"Mixed-acting means both mechanisms: ephedrine binds and activates α1, α2, β1 and β2 and also increases norepinephrine release. The result is massive sympathetic activation (constricted vessels, a working heart, continuous norepinephrine), which caused strokes and heart attacks and ended its use in US dietary supplements.","quote":"You can see that this drug not only can bind and activate all the receptors but also increase the release of norepinephrine. So we have this massive activation of your sympathetic nervous system, which causes people to have a heart attack","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 9; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-020","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"class","skill":"tell","multi":true,"concept":"indirect-acting-group","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which drugs are indirect-acting adrenergic agonists? Select all that apply.","options":[{"t":"Amphetamine","correct":true,"why":"Amphetamine pushes norepinephrine (and dopamine, serotonin) out of the terminal onto the receptors without binding them: indirect, a releasing agent."},{"t":"Cocaine","correct":true,"why":"Cocaine blocks the norepinephrine transporter (NET), so released norepinephrine stays in the synapse longer: indirect, a reuptake inhibitor."},{"t":"Phenelzine","correct":true,"why":"Phenelzine blocks monoamine oxidase (MAO) A and B irreversibly, so more norepinephrine is stored and released: indirect."},{"t":"Selegiline","correct":true,"why":"Selegiline blocks MAO-B irreversibly, so less catecholamine is broken down: indirect, acting on the enzyme and not on the receptor."},{"t":"Phenylephrine","correct":false,"why":"A direct, selective α1 agonist."},{"t":"Clonidine","correct":false,"why":"A direct, selective α2 agonist."},{"t":"Oxymetazoline","correct":false,"why":"A direct, non-selective α1 and α2 agonist."}],"teach":"Indirect-acting drugs do not bind the adrenergic receptor; they raise norepinephrine. Three groups: releasing agents (amphetamine), reuptake inhibitors (cocaine) and monoamine oxidase (MAO) inhibitors (phenelzine, selegiline). Direct agonists (phenylephrine, clonidine, oxymetazoline) bind the receptor themselves.","quote":"The other ones over here that are indirectly we have the amphetamine-like drugs. ... We have drugs like cocaine that are used recreationally ... And then we have some of the MAO inhibitors that by blocking the enzyme that breaks down catecholamines, we can increase the levels in the brain","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 9; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-021","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","level":1,"topic":"L10","sub":"indirect","skill":"apply","concept":"cocaine-nasal-vasoconstriction","tags":["in-class"],"source":"both","stem":"A person who snorts cocaine has repeated nosebleeds and damage to the nasal septum. Which mechanism explains it?","options":[{"t":"Excess α1 vasoconstriction","correct":true,"why":"Blocking the NET raises synaptic norepinephrine, which constricts nasal vessels through α1 so much that the mucosa dies."},{"t":"More acetylcholine on M3 → vasodilation","correct":false,"why":"Cocaine acts on norepinephrine reuptake, not acetylcholine."},{"t":"β2 activation → vasodilation","correct":false,"why":"The damage comes from too much constriction, not dilation."},{"t":"α2 activation → less norepinephrine","correct":false,"why":"Cocaine raises synaptic norepinephrine; it does not lower it."},{"t":"MAO block → tyramine crisis","correct":false,"why":"Monoamine oxidase (MAO) block is phenelzine and selegiline; cocaine blocks the norepinephrine transporter (NET)."}],"teach":"Cocaine is a reuptake inhibitor: it blocks the norepinephrine transporter (NET), raising synaptic norepinephrine and activating the postsynaptic receptors. In nasal blood vessels the α1 vasoconstriction is severe enough to kill the mucosa and destroy the septum, so users bleed from the nose. In the central nervous system it causes excitation; in the heart, higher heart rate and risk of a heart attack.","quote":"You're releasing norepinephrine, you're vasoconstricting those blood vessels so much that it starts to kill your mucosa of your sinus and destroy your septum and everything else in there. And that's gonna lead to bleeding on their nose. So, in the blood vessels, this drug can cause severe vasoconstriction.","note":"He asked the class “do you know why that is happening?” about the nosebleeds and answered himself.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 10; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-022","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","level":2,"topic":"L10","sub":"indirect","skill":"apply","concept":"cocaine-lidocaine-local","tags":[],"source":"transcript","lowYield":true,"stem":"Cocaine or another strong vasoconstrictor is sometimes added to lidocaine when a cut is sutured. What is the rationale?","options":[{"t":"Keeps lidocaine local","correct":true,"why":"Vasoconstriction decreases bleeding and stops lidocaine from going systemic and reaching the heart, where it can cause arrhythmias."},{"t":"Speeds lidocaine's spread to the heart","correct":false,"why":"The goal is the opposite: keep lidocaine out of the heart."},{"t":"Blocks the pain by dilating vessels","correct":false,"why":"The added drug constricts, not dilates, the vessels."},{"t":"Treats an arrhythmia caused by the cut","correct":false,"why":"The vasoconstrictor prevents lidocaine-induced arrhythmias; it does not treat one."}],"teach":"Lidocaine numbs the area. Adding a powerful vasoconstrictor (cocaine, or epinephrine/norepinephrine) constricts local vessels, which decreases bleeding during suturing and keeps lidocaine from being distributed systemically, where it could reach the heart and cause arrhythmias.","quote":"But the reason why we can add that cocaine or powerful vasoconstrictor is that we don't want it to become systemic because the lidocaine can stop your heart. So you want to stay local. So by vasoconstricting those blood vessels, you decrease the bleeding while you're suturing, and also you prevent the systemic distribution of our drug into the heart.","note":"A use, which he generally does not test (10/5: “I'm not testing you on the use”), so marked low yield. The drug list instead says cocaine is “used with lidocaine to control arrythmias (Na+ channel blocker)”; he said the vasoconstrictor keeps lidocaine away from the heart to prevent arrhythmias. Keyed to the transcript (see DL2 conflicts).","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 10; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-023","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"indirect","skill":"tell","concept":"amphetamine-primary-moa","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"What is the primary mechanism of action of dextroamphetamine and methylphenidate?","options":[{"t":"Presynaptic catecholamine release","correct":true,"why":"Primary action: release of norepinephrine, dopamine and serotonin."},{"t":"Fully block the norepinephrine transporter","correct":false,"why":"Full block of the norepinephrine transporter (NET) is cocaine’s mechanism; amphetamines leave the transporter mostly working and only moderately slow reuptake as a secondary effect."},{"t":"Irreversibly block monoamine oxidase B","correct":false,"why":"Irreversible MAO-B block is selegiline’s mechanism; amphetamines block monoamine oxidase (MAO) only moderately and as a secondary effect."},{"t":"Directly activate α1 receptors","correct":false,"why":"Direct α1 activation is phenylephrine’s mechanism; amphetamines do not typically bind and activate the receptors, they push norepinephrine out of the terminal onto them."},{"t":"Directly activate α2 receptors","correct":false,"why":"Direct α2 (Gi) activation is clonidine’s mechanism and lowers norepinephrine release; amphetamines raise it."}],"teach":"The amphetamine-like stimulants (dextroamphetamine, amphetamine, lisdexamfetamine, methylphenidate, dexmethylphenidate) work mainly by stimulating presynaptic release of norepinephrine (NE), dopamine (DA) and serotonin (5HT). Secondarily they moderately decrease reuptake and block monoamine oxidase (MAO).","quote":"So they do not work typically by binding and activating the receptors. They are indirectly enhancing the release, so they stimulate the release of catecholamine, such as norepinephrine, serotonin, and dopamine, OK?","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 11; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-024","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"indirect","skill":"recall","multi":true,"concept":"amphetamine-secondary-moa","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Besides stimulating catecholamine release, which weaker actions do the amphetamine-like stimulants have? Select all that apply.","options":[{"t":"Decrease reuptake of norepinephrine","correct":true,"why":"Second, moderate action: decrease reuptake (weak NET inhibition)."},{"t":"Block monoamine oxidase (MAO)","correct":true,"why":"Second, moderate action: blocks MAO, the enzyme that breaks catecholamines down."},{"t":"Activate presynaptic α2","correct":false,"why":"Activating α2 would lower release; that is clonidine."},{"t":"Block postsynaptic α1","correct":false,"why":"Blocking α1 is prazosin."},{"t":"Activate COMT","correct":false,"why":"Catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT) breaks norepinephrine down; activating it would lower synaptic norepinephrine, the opposite of a stimulant."}],"teach":"Primary mechanism: release of norepinephrine, dopamine and serotonin. Secondary, weak-to-moderate mechanisms: inhibition of reuptake and inhibition of monoamine oxidase (MAO). The drug touches several steps of norepinephrine handling.","quote":"You also have a secondary effect of being a very weaker inhibitor of the reuptake and the enzyme that breaks down catecholamine. So it has multiple mechanisms of action. The primary is releasing of norepinephrine with a very weak to moderate inhibition of the reuptake as well as the breakdown of catecholamines.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 11; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-025","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","level":1,"topic":"L10","sub":"indirect","skill":"apply","multi":true,"concept":"stimulant-effects","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"A patient takes increasing doses of dextroamphetamine. Which effects are expected? Select all that apply.","options":[{"t":"Decreased appetite","correct":true,"why":"Central effect of more norepinephrine and dopamine in the brain: less sleep and less appetite."},{"t":"Tremor","correct":true,"why":"Peripheral effect of higher sympathetic outflow: activated β2 receptors cause tremor."},{"t":"Increased heart rate","correct":true,"why":"Increased sympathetic outflow raises heart rate and blood pressure."},{"t":"Sedation","correct":false,"why":"Stimulants increase energy and decrease sleep."},{"t":"Bradycardia","correct":false,"why":"More sympathetic tone raises, not lowers, heart rate."}],"teach":"Stimulants raise sympathetic outflow. Central: mood, more energy, less sleep and appetite, better focus and alertness, faster breathing. Peripheral: tremor, sweating (diaphoresis), cardiovascular effects (higher blood pressure and heart rate) and hyperthermia. Side effects grow with dose.","quote":"So these drugs are gonna increase the sympathetic outflow as a side effect. So it may increase your blood pressure, increase your, your heart rate, it cause tremors, right, because you're activating those beta 2s","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 12; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-026","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","level":2,"topic":"L10","sub":"indirect","skill":"apply","concept":"stimulant-caffeine-ddi","tags":[],"source":"transcript","stem":"A student taking a prescribed stimulant drinks several caffeinated energy shots while studying overnight. Which drug–drug interaction is most likely?","options":[{"t":"Additive stimulant effects","correct":true,"why":"Caffeine is also a stimulant: two stimulants on board add up and push the dose toward side effects such as arrhythmias."},{"t":"Caffeine cancels the stimulant","correct":false,"why":"Both drugs stimulate; their effects add."},{"t":"No interaction","correct":false,"why":"Two stimulants together have an additive effect."},{"t":"Sedation from both drugs","correct":false,"why":"Both drugs raise catecholamine signaling (the stimulant by releasing norepinephrine and dopamine, caffeine as a stimulant), so the sum is more stimulation, not sedation."}],"teach":"Caffeine is a stimulant, so combining it with an amphetamine-like drug is additive: the therapeutic window shrinks toward the side-effect end. For example, a student drinking energy shots all night before an exam on top of a stimulant ended up in the emergency room with arrhythmias.","quote":"Caffeine is a stimulant. So if you are taking a stimulant and then you add caffeine and Red Bull in 5 minutes, you know, whatever crap that is, uh, you're just compounding. ... She was in the ER because she was having arrhythmias.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 12; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-027","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"mao","skill":"tell","concept":"mao-a-vs-b-location","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which statement about the two monoamine oxidase (MAO) subtypes is CORRECT?","options":[{"t":"MAO-B is mainly in the brain","correct":true,"why":"MAO-B is present in the CNS striatum and breaks down dopamine (“B for brain”)."},{"t":"MAO-A is only in the brain","correct":false,"why":"MAO-A is in the brain, liver and gut; its gut location is why non-selective inhibitors cause the tyramine reaction."},{"t":"MAO-B is mainly in the gut","correct":false,"why":"The gut enzyme is MAO-A."},{"t":"MAO-B breaks down acetylcholine","correct":false,"why":"MAO breaks down catecholamines (norepinephrine, dopamine), not acetylcholine."}],"teach":"Both monoamine oxidase (MAO) subtypes break down catecholamines. MAO-A is in the brain, liver and gastrointestinal tract and breaks down norepinephrine and dopamine; MAO-B is in the central nervous system (striatum) and breaks down dopamine. Memory aid: B for brain.","quote":"So the older drugs. They are less selective, so they can inhibit both enzymes, the MAOA that is in the gut, as well as the MAOB that is for the brain. So if you're not sure, think about B for brain","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 13; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-028","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"mao","skill":"tell","concept":"phenelzine-vs-selegiline","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which of the following statements is CORRECT? (MAO = monoamine oxidase; COMT = catechol-O-methyltransferase)","options":[{"t":"Phenelzine blocks MAO-A and MAO-B","correct":true,"why":"Phenelzine is the non-selective MAO inhibitor: it blocks both monoamine oxidase (MAO) subtypes."},{"t":"Selegiline blocks MAO-A and MAO-B","correct":false,"why":"Selegiline is selective for MAO-B (at low doses)."},{"t":"Phenelzine blocks MAO-B only","correct":false,"why":"Phenelzine is non-selective."},{"t":"Selegiline blocks the norepinephrine transporter","correct":false,"why":"Blocking the norepinephrine transporter is cocaine."},{"t":"Phenelzine blocks COMT","correct":false,"why":"Phenelzine blocks monoamine oxidase (both subtypes), not catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT)."}],"teach":"Phenelzine: non-selective inhibitor of monoamine oxidase A and B (MAO-A and MAO-B), irreversible. Selegiline (low doses): selective MAO-B inhibitor, irreversible. Both raise catecholamines by blocking their breakdown.","quote":"There are MOA inhibitors. We have the non-selectives, and I picked phenelzine as our prototypical drug. ... And then we have a better MAO inhibitor, uh, selegiline, which is selective to the MAOB.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 14; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-029","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"mao","skill":"recall","concept":"maoi-irreversible","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Phenelzine and selegiline bind monoamine oxidase (MAO):","options":[{"t":"Irreversibly","correct":true,"why":"Both are irreversible antagonists: once bound, the enzyme is out and the body has to make new ones."},{"t":"Reversibly and competitively","correct":false,"why":"Both are irreversible: once bound, that enzyme stays blocked until the body makes new enzyme."},{"t":"As partial agonists","correct":false,"why":"They block (antagonize) the enzyme; they do not activate it."},{"t":"Phenelzine reversibly, selegiline irreversibly","correct":false,"why":"Both are irreversible."}],"teach":"Phenelzine and selegiline are both irreversible antagonists of monoamine oxidase (MAO). Once the drug binds, that enzyme molecule no longer works; activity returns only when the body makes new enzyme.","quote":"This drug is an irreversible antagonist, which means is, once you bind that enzyme, your body has to make new ones. That one is, is toasted, right?","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 14; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-030","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","level":2,"ladder":"mao-inhibitor","ladderName":"MAO inhibitor, tyramine and the hypertensive crisis","topic":"L10","sub":"mao","skill":"apply","concept":"cheese-effect-crisis","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"A patient taking phenelzine eats aged cheese and drinks wine at a party. Which is most likely?","options":[{"t":"Hypertensive crisis","correct":true,"why":"Non-selective MAO inhibition leaves dietary tyramine unprocessed: the “cheese effect”, a hypertensive crisis."},{"t":"Severe hypotension","correct":false,"why":"The tyramine reaction raises blood pressure."},{"t":"Bradycardia from vagal tone","correct":false,"why":"The reaction is sympathetic and hypertensive."},{"t":"No reaction","correct":false,"why":"Fermented foods are high in tyramine, which the patient cannot break down."},{"t":"Sedation","correct":false,"why":"The tyramine reaction is sympathetic: blood pressure climbs to a hypertensive crisis; it does not sedate."}],"teach":"A non-selective monoamine oxidase (MAO) inhibitor blocks MAO in the gut as well as the brain, so dietary tyramine from fermented foods (cheese, bread, wine) is not broken down. The result is a hypertensive crisis, the “cheese effect”. A patient on the drug who eats cheese can pass out from the very high pressure and need emergency care.","quote":"You cannot eat anything that is fermented. Like cheese and bread and wine, all of those are off the plate, right? And the reason being is because you have the inability to break down that dietary tyramine that you're consuming, and those fermented foods have high levels of tyramine, and that can cause what is called a hypertensive crisis.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 15; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-031","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","level":2,"ladder":"mao-inhibitor","ladderName":"MAO inhibitor, tyramine and the hypertensive crisis","topic":"L10","sub":"mao","skill":"tell","concept":"no-cheese-effect-mao-b","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which monoamine oxidase inhibitor carries no “cheese effect” with dietary tyramine?","options":[{"t":"Selegiline","correct":true,"why":"Selective MAO-B inhibition acts in the brain only, so gut tyramine is still broken down: no cheese effect."},{"t":"Phenelzine","correct":false,"why":"Phenelzine also blocks gut MAO-A, so tyramine causes a hypertensive crisis."},{"t":"Both phenelzine and selegiline","correct":false,"why":"Only the non-selective drug causes the cheese effect."},{"t":"Neither drug","correct":false,"why":"Selegiline is the one without it."}],"teach":"No cheese effect for selective MAO-B inhibitors: selegiline blocks monoamine oxidase B in the brain and leaves gut MAO-A free to break down dietary tyramine. So, when cost is not an issue, the selective MAO-B inhibitor is preferred.","quote":"So, if we need to choose one and your patient, money is not an issue, I'd rather pick the selective MOAB because that only blocks in the brain and you don't have to worry about the cheese effect associated with your patients, OK?","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 15; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-032","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","level":1,"ladder":"mao-inhibitor","ladderName":"MAO inhibitor, tyramine and the hypertensive crisis","topic":"L10","sub":"mao","skill":"apply","concept":"maoi-more-ne-stored","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Blocking monoamine oxidase (MAO) is most likely to increase norepinephrine release by:","options":[{"t":"Leaving more stored in the vesicles","correct":true,"why":"With less breakdown, more norepinephrine is stored, so each release puts more into the synapse."},{"t":"Blocking the α2 autoreceptor","correct":false,"why":"Blocking the α2 autoreceptor (Gi) removes the brake on release; that is mirtazapine’s mechanism, and an MAO inhibitor leaves α2 alone."},{"t":"Making the transporter pump more out","correct":false,"why":"The transporter (NET) pumps norepinephrine back in, it does not push it out; an MAO inhibitor acts on the breakdown enzyme inside the terminal and leaves the transporter alone."},{"t":"Stopping the synthesis from tyrosine","correct":false,"why":"Synthesis continues; it is breakdown that is reduced."}],"teach":"Monoamine oxidase breaks down norepinephrine, dopamine and epinephrine. Blocking it means more norepinephrine is stored in the vesicles, so more is released into the synapse. Raising brain catecholamines this way is how the MAO inhibitors, the oldest antidepressants, decrease depression.","quote":"As such, if I block the enzyme, I can make an assumption that I'm gonna have a lot more norepinephrine being stored in the vesicles, which means that when I release, I have more norepinephrine being released.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slides 13–14; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-033","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"a1","skill":"recall","multi":true,"concept":"alpha1-sites","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which are major sites of action of α1 receptors? Select all that apply.","options":[{"t":"Eye (pupil)","correct":true,"why":"“I see”: α1 dilates the pupil."},{"t":"Blood vessels of the nose","correct":true,"why":"“and smell”: α1 constricts nasal vessels (decongestion)."},{"t":"Arteries and veins","correct":true,"why":"“bloody”: α1 sets afterload and preload."},{"t":"Urethra (bladder sphincter, prostate vessels)","correct":true,"why":"“urine”: α1 on the sphincter and the blood vessels around the prostate."},{"t":"Sinoatrial node","correct":false,"why":"The heart is run by β1."},{"t":"Bronchial smooth muscle","correct":false,"why":"The lungs have M3 (parasympathetic only) and β2; α1 is not a lung receptor."}],"teach":"Memory aid for α1 sites: “I see and smell bloody urine”: eye (pupil dilation), nose (nasal blood vessels), blood vessels (systemic arteries for afterload, veins for preload) and urethra (bladder sphincter and the blood vessels of the prostate). α1 is Gq: phospholipase C, IP3, more Ca++.","quote":"So, the way I remember when I was in school is I see and smell bloody urine, right? ... So think about, uh, those sites as our major sites of action.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slides 16–17; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-034","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","level":1,"topic":"L10","sub":"a1","skill":"apply","concept":"phenylephrine-eye","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Phenylephrine drops are placed in the eye. Which effect is most likely?","options":[{"t":"Mydriasis","correct":true,"why":"α1 on the radial (dilator) fibers contracts and pulls the pupil open: pupil dilation."},{"t":"Miosis","correct":false,"why":"Miosis is the M3 effect on the circular muscle."},{"t":"Lacrimation","correct":false,"why":"Lacrimation is a muscarinic (DUMBBELSS) effect."},{"t":"No effect on the pupil","correct":false,"why":"α1 is one of the two receptors controlling pupil size."}],"teach":"The pupil has dual innervation: M3 on the circular muscle constricts it, α1 on the radial muscle dilates it. Phenylephrine, a selective α1 agonist (Gq, ↑Ca++), contracts the radial fibers and causes mydriasis (pupil dilation). Burning and blurred vision are its eye side effects.","quote":"So if activate the alpha 1 is in the eyes, they are in these transverse fibers, so we're going to pull them apart by constricting and you're going to get pupil dilation, also known as madriasis, right?","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 18; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-035","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","level":1,"topic":"L10","sub":"a1","skill":"apply","concept":"phenylephrine-preload-afterload","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Phenylephrine reaches the systemic arteries and veins. Which change is most likely?","options":[{"t":"Preload and afterload both rise","correct":true,"why":"α1 in arterial smooth muscle raises afterload; α1 in venous smooth muscle raises preload; blood pressure goes up."},{"t":"Preload and afterload both fall","correct":false,"why":"Both falling is what an α1 antagonist (prazosin) does: without Gq signaling in arteries and veins, afterload and preload drop. Phenylephrine activates α1 and raises both."},{"t":"Only preload rises","correct":false,"why":"The arteries also constrict, so afterload rises too."},{"t":"Only afterload rises","correct":false,"why":"The veins also constrict, so preload rises too."}],"teach":"Phenylephrine activates α1 (Gq, ↑Ca++) in vascular smooth muscle: arterial constriction raises afterload, venous constriction raises preload, and blood pressure rises. That is why it is used carefully in patients with hypertension; long use can itself keep blood pressure high.","quote":"So increase calcium. In the blood vessels, it's going to increase both preload and after load, so it's going to increase your blood pressure. And we're going to be careful with patients with hypertension.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 18; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-036","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"a1","skill":"recall","multi":true,"concept":"phenylephrine-adr","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which adverse effects of phenylephrine should a patient be warned about? Select all that apply.","options":[{"t":"Burning","correct":true,"why":"Anything put in the eye or nose can burn; burning is a listed adverse reaction."},{"t":"Rebound congestion","correct":true,"why":"Used for more than 3 days, the nasal decongestant gives rebound congestion."},{"t":"Blurred vision","correct":true,"why":"Used in the eye, α1 activation dilates the pupil, and vision blurs."},{"t":"Higher blood pressure","correct":true,"why":"Careful in patients with hypertension: α1 raises preload and afterload."},{"t":"Dry mouth","correct":false,"why":"Dry mouth is the complaint with clonidine."},{"t":"Orthostatic hypotension","correct":false,"why":"That is the α1 antagonists (prazosin); phenylephrine raises pressure."}],"teach":"Phenylephrine's adverse reactions: burning, blurred vision and rebound congestion (do not use for more than 3 days), and higher blood pressure, so care is needed in patients with hypertension. Each follows from α1 activation at its sites.","quote":"And then there are some side effects associated with that, which of course if you put anything in the eye, it's going to cause some burning, blurred vision, uh, you may have rebound congestion. As such, if you read in the back of the box, it tells you you don't use for more than 3 days.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 18; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-037","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"a1","skill":"tell","concept":"oxymetazoline-less-constriction","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which of the following statements about oxymetazoline, a nasal decongestant, is CORRECT?","options":[{"t":"Its α2 (Gi) action offsets part of the α1 effect","correct":true,"why":"It is non-selective: α1 (Gq) constricts, α2 (Gi) relaxes, so the two partly offset."},{"t":"It blocks α1 receptors in the nasal vessels","correct":false,"why":"Oxymetazoline activates α1 (Gq, Ca++ up, constriction); a drug that blocked α1 would dilate the nasal vessels and worsen congestion."},{"t":"It activates β2 receptors in the nasal vessels","correct":false,"why":"β2 (Gs) would relax the vessels; oxymetazoline binds α1 and α2 only."},{"t":"It acts indirectly, by releasing norepinephrine from the nerve","correct":false,"why":"It binds and activates the α receptors itself; an indirect drug (amphetamine, cocaine) would work through the nerve’s own norepinephrine."}],"teach":"Oxymetazoline is a non-selective α1 and α2 agonist acting on nasal smooth muscle. α1 (Gq) constricts and α2 (Gi) relaxes, so the two receptors “play with each other” and the vessels constrict less than with a pure α1 agonist.","quote":"This is a non-selective alpha 1, alpha 2, so they're gonna kind of, you know, play with each other. Remember, alpha 1s are Q constriction. Alpha 2 is I relaxing, right? So you don't have as much constriction of those blood vessels that you would expect.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 19; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-038","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"a1","skill":"tell","concept":"alpha1-vs-alpha2-messenger","tags":["in-class"],"source":"both","stem":"Which statement about α1 and α2 activation is CORRECT?","options":[{"t":"α1 raises Ca++; α2 lowers cAMP","correct":true,"why":"α1 is Gq (calcium goes up, excitation); α2 is Gi (cAMP goes down, inhibition)."},{"t":"α1 lowers cAMP; α2 raises Ca++","correct":false,"why":"This swaps the G proteins: α1 is Gq (Ca++ up, constriction), α2 is Gi (cAMP down, inhibition)."},{"t":"Both raise cAMP","correct":false,"why":"Raising cAMP is Gs, the β receptors; neither α receptor is Gs."},{"t":"Both are Gq","correct":false,"why":"α2 is coupled to Gi (cAMP down, inhibition); only α1 is Gq (Ca++ up). If both were Gq, clonidine could not lower sympathetic output."}],"teach":"A student asked why α1 and α2 agonists seem to contradict each other. They do: α1 is Gαq and excitatory (Ca++ up, constriction), α2 is Gαi and inhibitory (cyclic AMP, cAMP, down; relaxation and less norepinephrine release).","quote":"They do. Because alpha 1 agonist constrict, alpha 2 relax because one is exciting, the other one is inhibiting. Remember, alpha 2 is G alpha I, alpha 1 is G alpha Q. So calcium goes up with alpha 1s, scamp goes down with the alpha 2s.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slides 17, 21; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-039","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"a2","skill":"recall","concept":"alpha2-location-cns","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Where are the α2 receptors that clonidine acts on primarily located?","options":[{"t":"Nerve endings in the CNS","correct":true,"why":"α2 is primarily in the central nervous system (CNS), presynaptically, modulating norepinephrine release."},{"t":"Ventricular muscle of the heart","correct":false,"why":"The heart is run by β1."},{"t":"Detrusor muscle of the bladder","correct":false,"why":"The bladder's adrenergic receptor stressed is β3."},{"t":"Bronchial smooth muscle","correct":false,"why":"The lungs have M3 and β2."},{"t":"Radial muscle of the iris","correct":false,"why":"The iris dilator is α1."}],"teach":"α2 receptors are located at nerve endings, primarily in the central nervous system (CNS), and also in the gastrointestinal tract and on pancreatic β cells. Presynaptically they modulate how much norepinephrine is released; they are Gi (less cAMP), so they are inhibitory.","quote":"So alpha chews are going to be primary locations going to be the CNS. That's what we're going to be thinking about those guys. They are going to be pre-synaptically, so they're modulating the levels of norepinephrine release.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slides 20–21; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-040","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"a2","skill":"tell","concept":"clonidine-agonist-inhibits","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Clonidine:","options":[{"t":"Agonist at an inhibitory Gi-coupled receptor","correct":true,"why":"α2 is Gi: activating it suppresses norepinephrine release, so the sympathetic system is turned down."},{"t":"Antagonist at α1 receptors","correct":false,"why":"An α1 antagonist (prazosin) lowers pressure by removing Gq constriction in the vessels; clonidine binds α2 and activates it."},{"t":"Blocker of the norepinephrine transporter","correct":false,"why":"Blocking the norepinephrine transporter (cocaine) leaves more norepinephrine in the synapse and raises sympathetic activity, the opposite of clonidine."},{"t":"Irreversible inhibitor of monoamine oxidase (MAO)","correct":false,"why":"Irreversible MAO block (phenelzine, selegiline) lets more norepinephrine be stored and released, raising sympathetic activity; clonidine does not touch the enzyme."}],"teach":"Clonidine is a selective α2 agonist. Because α2 is coupled to Gαi, activating it is inhibitory: less cAMP, less norepinephrine release and less sympathetic outflow. It behaves like an antagonist of the sympathetic system but is an agonist at its receptor.","quote":"So clonidine is an agonist that activates the inhibitory pathway. So he's behaving like an antagonist, but he's actually an agonist.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slides 21, 23–24; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-041","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","level":1,"ladder":"clonidine","ladderName":"Clonidine: α2, an add-on, and stopping it","topic":"L10","sub":"a2","skill":"apply","multi":true,"concept":"clonidine-effects","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which effects does clonidine produce? Select all that apply.","options":[{"t":"Decreased sympathetic tone","correct":true,"why":"Central α2 (Gi) activation suppresses norepinephrine release."},{"t":"Vasodilation","correct":true,"why":"Less sympathetic input to α1 in arteries and veins: they relax (less afterload and preload)."},{"t":"Bradycardia","correct":true,"why":"Less sympathetic drive to the heart lowers heart rate and contractile force."},{"t":"Tachycardia","correct":false,"why":"Heart rate falls, not rises."},{"t":"More norepinephrine release","correct":false,"why":"More release comes from blocking the α2 autoreceptor (mirtazapine); clonidine activates it, and Gi then suppresses release."},{"t":"Higher preload","correct":false,"why":"Venous sympathetic tone falls, so preload falls."}],"teach":"Clonidine's effects are decrease of sympathetic tone, vasodilation and bradycardia. Suppressing sympathetic input to the heart lowers heart rate and force (cardiac output); to the arteries, afterload; to the veins, preload.","quote":"So, if I suppress sympathetic to the heart, I'm going to decrease the heart rate and the contractile force. If I suppress the sympathetics to the arteries, I'm going to decrease the afterload. If I suppress the sympathetics to the veins, I'm going to decrease the preload, plus everything else.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slides 23–24; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-043","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","level":3,"ladder":"clonidine","ladderName":"Clonidine: α2, an add-on, and stopping it","topic":"L10","sub":"a2","skill":"apply","concept":"clonidine-withdrawal-crisis","tags":["exam-cue"],"source":"both","stem":"A patient stops clonidine abruptly after months of use. Which is most likely, and why?","options":[{"t":"Hypertensive crisis from up-regulated receptors","correct":true,"why":"Chronic suppression of sympathetic tone makes the body add receptors; without the drug, the released norepinephrine acts on many more receptors."},{"t":"Hypotension from down-regulated α1 receptors","correct":false,"why":"Chronic suppression up-regulates receptors, and the result is high, not low, pressure."},{"t":"Bradycardia from extra parasympathetic tone","correct":false,"why":"Stopping the drug ends the suppression of the sympathetic system, so heart rate rises rather than falls."},{"t":"Bronchoconstriction from unopposed parasympathetic tone","correct":false,"why":"Withdrawal unleashes sympathetic activity; it does not constrict the airways."}],"teach":"Months of suppressed sympathetic tone cause receptor up-regulation. Stopping clonidine cold turkey leaves many receptors and a surge of norepinephrine: a hypertensive crisis. The major counseling point: patients must be weaned off, not stopped abruptly.","quote":"We're gonna make more receptors, right? We're gonna cause up regulation. So what is the one education point that you have to make to your patients? You got to wean yourself off because if you go cold turkey, you got all these receptors, now norepinephrine is flowing like there's no tomorrow, and you go into a hypertensive crisis, right?","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 26; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-044","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","level":1,"topic":"L10","sub":"a2","skill":"apply","concept":"clonidine-constipation","tags":["in-class"],"source":"both","stem":"Clonidine is most likely to cause constipation by:","options":[{"t":"Letting less acetylcholine reach gut M3","correct":true,"why":"α2 on the cholinergic nerve slows acetylcholine secretion, so M3 is not activated and motility and tone fall."},{"t":"Blocking gut M3 receptors","correct":false,"why":"Clonidine has no affinity for M3; it acts one step earlier, on the α2 (Gi) receptor of the parasympathetic nerve, so less acetylcholine is released onto M3."},{"t":"Activating gut α1 receptors","correct":false,"why":"Clonidine binds α2, not α1; α1 (Gq) is not the receptor that sets gut motility."},{"t":"Raising norepinephrine release in the gut wall","correct":false,"why":"Activating α2 (Gi) on the sympathetic terminal lowers norepinephrine release; the constipation comes from less acetylcholine, not more norepinephrine."}],"teach":"The gut is one of the dual-innervation exceptions: α2 receptors slow the release of acetylcholine. Activating them with clonidine means less acetylcholine, no M3 activation, less motility and tone, and constipation.","quote":"What would you predict that would happen in the gut? Well, it was kind of list their constipation, right? But why? Because you're slowing down. Because you're slowing down the secretion of acetylcholine, this is one of our dual innervation exception, right? That by suppressing acetylcholine release, we don't activate the M3s","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 26; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-045","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"a2","skill":"recall","multi":true,"concept":"clonidine-adr","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which adverse effects are expected with clonidine? Select all that apply.","options":[{"t":"Sedation","correct":true,"why":"Central nervous system inhibition; CNS side effects are the most common."},{"t":"Hypotension","correct":true,"why":"Too much of the wanted effect; with bradycardia, it raises the risk of falls."},{"t":"Sexual dysfunction","correct":true,"why":"Sexual function needs both divisions working together; clonidine suppresses the sympathetic side."},{"t":"Nightmares and depression","correct":true,"why":"Central nervous system (CNS) inhibition also brings nightmares and depression."},{"t":"Tachycardia","correct":false,"why":"Clonidine causes bradycardia."},{"t":"Diarrhea","correct":false,"why":"Clonidine causes constipation."}],"teach":"Clonidine's adverse reactions: CNS effects (sedation, nightmares, depression), dry mouth and constipation, hypotension and bradycardia (risk of falls), sexual dysfunction, and a hypertensive crisis on abrupt withdrawal from receptor up-regulation. Each follows from suppressed sympathetic outflow; the constipation comes from less acetylcholine released onto gut M3.","quote":"So when it comes to side effects, the most common side effects with this drug can be sedation because you're inhibiting the CNS. Patients may complain of nightmares and maybe depression. Dry mouth is very common. ... so hypertension and bradycardia can be severe if the dose is too high. It may cause sexual dysfunction.","note":"The transcript has “hypertension and bradycardia” where the slide says “Hypotension & Bradycardia”; in context (“lower blood pressure, lower heart rate”) it is hypotension. Keyed to the slide.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 26; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-046","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"a2","skill":"tell","concept":"idine-osin-names","tags":["alpha"],"source":"both","stem":"A drug name ends in “-idine” (for example, brimonidine or tizanidine). Which mechanism should you predict?","options":[{"t":"α2 agonist","correct":true,"why":"Most drugs ending in -idine are α2 agonists like clonidine."},{"t":"Selective α1 antagonist","correct":false,"why":"The α1 antagonists end in -osin (prazosin, terazosin)."},{"t":"Muscarinic antagonist","correct":false,"why":"Atropine-like drugs carry “trop”."},{"t":"Cholinesterase inhibitor","correct":false,"why":"Those end in -stigmine."},{"t":"α1 agonist","correct":false,"why":"Phenylephrine “sounds like” epinephrine and norepinephrine."}],"teach":"Name cues: -idine (clonidine, brimonidine, tizanidine) → α2 agonist; -osin (prazosin, terazosin, doxazosin) → selective α1 antagonist; phenylephrine sounds like epinephrine and norepinephrine → α1 agonist. Clonidine is the prototype α2 agonist.","quote":"It doesn't matter if it's clonidine or brimonidine or, you know, tizanidine. They all have this edine with it, and it's gonna tell you where they're gonna fall, OK? So for our purpose on exam one, we're gonna focus on clonidine because that's our prototypical drug.","note":"“exam one” in the quote is a slip for Exam 2, the exam this lecture belongs to.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slides 17, 22; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-047","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","level":1,"topic":"L10","sub":"a2","skill":"apply","concept":"clonidine-everywhere","tags":["in-class"],"source":"both","stem":"If your patient on clonidine has a lower blood pressure and a dry mouth, both effects come from:","options":[{"t":"Less sympathetic outflow","correct":true,"why":"One central action reduces sympathetic input to every organ: salivary glands, heart, arteries and veins."},{"t":"M3 block in the glands and vessels","correct":false,"why":"Clonidine has no affinity for M3; the salivary glands dry because their sympathetic input (which, with the parasympathetic, drives salivation) is suppressed."},{"t":"α1 activation in the glands and vessels","correct":false,"why":"Activating α1 would constrict vessels and raise pressure."},{"t":"More norepinephrine at every synapse","correct":false,"why":"Activating the α2 (Gi) autoreceptor lowers norepinephrine release; more norepinephrine would raise pressure."}],"teach":"A student asked how clonidine can do both. Answer: the sympathetic system is one-to-many, so suppressing it centrally suppresses salivation, pupil dilation, heart rate and force, and vessel tone at once. The arteries and veins have only sympathetic innervation, so without it they relax and blood pressure falls.","quote":"Because it's suppressed sympathetic to all over the body. So remember the sympathetic is one too many, right? So by suppressing the sympathetic outflow, you're gonna suppress the salivation. ... There's only sympathetic to the arteries and the veins, right?","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slides 23–24, 26; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-048","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"a1ant","skill":"recall","concept":"alpha-blocker-irreversible","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which α-receptor antagonist is irreversible?","options":[{"t":"Phenoxybenzamine","correct":true,"why":"Phenoxybenzamine is the non-selective (α1 and α2) irreversible antagonist; all the others are reversible."},{"t":"Prazosin","correct":false,"why":"Prazosin is a reversible, selective α1 antagonist."},{"t":"Terazosin","correct":false,"why":"An -osin: reversible, selective α1."},{"t":"None; all are reversible","correct":false,"why":"Phenoxybenzamine is the irreversible one."},{"t":"Mirtazapine","correct":false,"why":"Mirtazapine is a reversible α2 (and H1, muscarinic, α1) antagonist."}],"teach":"The α antagonists: irreversible, non-selective α1 and α2: phenoxybenzamine. Reversible, α1 selective: prazosin, terazosin, doxazosin, alfuzosin, tamsulosin, silodosin (the -osins). Reversible, α2 non-selective: mirtazapine (α2, H1, muscarinic, α1).","quote":"So all the other drugs are gonna be reversible and then we have here alpha-1 selectives all the zosin or Osin, right?","note":"He described phenoxybenzamine aloud (“a non-selective alpha 1 and alpha 2 irreversible antagonists”) while answering a student question, then said “we're gonna get a little bit ahead of us, so just wait”; its own slides (34–38) were not taught on 10/6.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 27; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-050","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","level":1,"topic":"L10","sub":"a1ant","skill":"apply","concept":"alpha1-block-vasodilation","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Blocking α1 receptors in a patient with hypertension lowers blood pressure through:","options":[{"t":"Less Gq signaling → less Ca++ → vasodilation","correct":true,"why":"Norepinephrine cannot bind α1, so less Gq, PLC and IP3, less calcium, and the vessels relax: lower TPR."},{"t":"More Gi signaling → less cAMP → vasodilation","correct":false,"why":"Gi lowering cyclic AMP (cAMP) describes α2 activation, not α1 blockade."},{"t":"Less β1 signaling → lower heart rate","correct":false,"why":"Prazosin does not block β1; heart rate actually rises by reflex."},{"t":"More nitric oxide from the endothelium → vasodilation","correct":false,"why":"Nitric oxide comes from endothelial M3; α1 block works on smooth-muscle calcium."}],"teach":"In the arteries and veins, α1 is the main receptor and the sympathetic system the only (and so the primary physiological) tone: they are always constricting through Gq → PLC → IP3 → Ca++. Blocking α1 lowers that calcium, the vessels dilate, and systemic vascular resistance (total peripheral resistance, TPR) falls.","quote":"if I block it, norepinephrine can't bind to that receptor. As such, I have less GQ. If I have less GQ, I'm gonna have less calcium, and if I have less calcium, I have more vasodilation.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 29; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-052","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","level":1,"ladder":"prazosin","ladderName":"Prazosin, norepinephrine and phenoxybenzamine","topic":"L10","sub":"a1ant","skill":"apply","concept":"alpha1-block-reflex-tachycardia","tags":["in-class"],"source":"both","stem":"Prazosin dilates the resistance arteries. Which response follows through the baroreceptors?","options":[{"t":"Reflex tachycardia (palpitations)","correct":true,"why":"The baroreceptors sense low pressure and raise sympathetic drive: reflex tachycardia, felt as palpitations."},{"t":"Reflex bradycardia","correct":false,"why":"Bradycardia is the reflex to a rise in pressure."},{"t":"Miosis","correct":false,"why":"Miosis is not a baroreflex response."},{"t":"Less sympathetic drive to the heart","correct":false,"why":"Low pressure increases sympathetic drive."}],"teach":"Dilating the arteries lowers blood pressure; the baroreceptors sense it and increase sympathetic outflow, giving reflex tachycardia (palpitations). Because of this, prazosin is not a good monotherapy; a β blocker may be added to block the tachycardia.","quote":"What about if it's the alpha ones in the arteries? If you vasodilate the arteries, now your barrels are gonna sense my blood pressure is too low. What is it gonna do? I'm gonna increase the sympathetic, I'm gonna get reflex tachycardia, so palpitations, right?","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 30; transcript 10/6","note":"The teach keeps his aside that prazosin is not a good monotherapy and that a β blocker is added for the tachycardia; it is in his 10/6 words: “So it’s not a really drug, a really good drug to use as a monotherapy, but you’re gonna have to add like a beta blocker to block the tachycardia.”"},{"id":"L10-053","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"a1ant","skill":"recall","multi":true,"concept":"prazosin-adr","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which adverse effects are expected when a patient starts prazosin? Select all that apply.","options":[{"t":"First-dose orthostatic hypotension","correct":true,"why":"First dose effect: orthostatic hypotension and syncope (30–90 min)."},{"t":"Reflex tachycardia","correct":true,"why":"Baroreceptors respond to the fall in pressure."},{"t":"Blurred vision","correct":true,"why":"α1 in the eye is blocked, so the pupil cannot dilate properly."},{"t":"Headache","correct":true,"why":"A common adverse reaction for vasodilators."},{"t":"Bradycardia","correct":false,"why":"Heart rate rises by reflex."},{"t":"Hypertensive crisis on withdrawal","correct":false,"why":"A hypertensive crisis on withdrawal follows an α2 agonist (clonidine): the receptors up-regulate and a stop lets norepinephrine hit them all. Stopping prazosin removes a block, not an agonist."},{"t":"Rebound congestion","correct":false,"why":"Rebound congestion follows an α1 agonist (phenylephrine) in the nose; prazosin blocks α1 and causes stuffiness, not rebound."}],"teach":"Prazosin's adverse reactions: the first-dose effect (orthostatic hypotension and syncope, 30–90 minutes), reflex tachycardia (palpitations), dizziness, blurred vision and headache. Headache and flushing are common to vasodilators.","quote":"So we have that first dose effect that is gonna cause the reflex tachycardia because your barrels are gonna sense that your blood pressure is too low. ... We have alpha ones in the eyes, so we won't be able to dilate very properly, so you get the blurred vision.","note":"The transcript says “first dose orthostatic hypertension” and “orthostatic hypertension, also known as syncope”; the slide says “Orthostatic Hypotension & Syncope”, and he describes patients passing out. Keyed to the slide (hypotension).","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 30; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-054","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"a1ant","skill":"apply","concept":"vasodilator-headache","tags":["prazosin"],"source":"both","stem":"A vasodilator such as prazosin is most likely to cause headache through:","options":[{"t":"Raised pressure inside the skull","correct":true,"why":"The brain is enclosed by the rigid skull; dilating its vessels raises pressure in the head (with flushing)."},{"t":"α2 block in the brain","correct":false,"why":"Prazosin binds α1, not α2, and the headache is a pressure effect of dilated vessels inside the skull, not a receptor effect in the brain."},{"t":"Constriction of the vessels in the brain","correct":false,"why":"An α1 blocker dilates; constriction (α1 activation) would lower the pressure in the head, not raise it."},{"t":"Less acetylcholine in the brain","correct":false,"why":"Prazosin has no affinity for cholinergic receptors or the enzyme; the headache is from dilated vessels in a rigid skull."}],"teach":"A general rule: a vasodilator most likely causes headache. Vessels in the face and brain dilate, and the brain sits inside the hard skull with little room, so pressure in the head rises; flushing goes with it.","quote":"So when you vasodilate those blood vessels, you increase the pressure in the head and you get the flushing and the headache associated with that. So one of the good general rules is if you are a vasodilator, most likely you're gonna get a headache for your patient, OK?","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 30; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-055","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","level":1,"topic":"L10","sub":"a1ant","skill":"apply","concept":"alpha1-antagonist-nose","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"What would an α1 antagonist do to nasal congestion?","options":[{"t":"Make it worse","correct":true,"why":"Blocking α1 relaxes the nasal blood vessels, which adds to the congestion."},{"t":"Clear it","correct":false,"why":"Clearing congestion needs vessel constriction, an α1 agonist (phenylephrine)."},{"t":"Cause rebound congestion only after 3 days","correct":false,"why":"Rebound congestion after days of use is the phenylephrine warning."},{"t":"Have no effect on the nose","correct":false,"why":"The nose is one of the α1 sites (“smell”)."}],"teach":"The nose is an α1 site. An α1 agonist (phenylephrine) constricts nasal vessels and relieves congestion; an α1 antagonist (prazosin) relaxes them and makes congestion worse, so α1 antagonists have no use as decongestants.","quote":"And then, uh, we don't really use alpha one antagonist to decongestant because it's gonna make it worse. It's gonna relax the blood vessels in the nose and it's gonna cause even more congestion for those patients, right?","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 28; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-056","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"a1ant","skill":"recall","concept":"arteries-veins-sympathetic-only","tags":["alpha1"],"source":"both","stem":"Which statement about the arteries and veins is CORRECT?","options":[{"t":"They have only sympathetic innervation","correct":true,"why":"Sympathetic is the only (primary physiological) tone, so α1 always keeps some constriction."},{"t":"They have only parasympathetic innervation","correct":false,"why":"Parasympathetic-only is the lungs, not the vessels."},{"t":"Their main receptor is β1","correct":false,"why":"Their main receptor is α1."},{"t":"They are relaxed unless a drug is given","correct":false,"why":"They are always constricting to some degree to maintain blood pressure."}],"teach":"The arteries and veins are one of the autonomic exceptions: sympathetic innervation only, with α1 as the predominant receptor. They are always constricting; the question is how much. Removing that sympathetic tone (clonidine centrally, prazosin at the receptor) relaxes them.","quote":"There's only sympathetic to the arteries and the veins, right? So, you're always constricting. The question is how much you're constricting. So, are you constricting a lot or not a lot. You have to constrict somehow in order to maintain your blood pressure. But if you don't have sympathetics, relax","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 29; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-057","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"mirt","skill":"apply","concept":"mirtazapine-inhibit-inhibitor","tags":["in-class"],"source":"both","stem":"Mirtazapine, a presynaptic α2 antagonist, increases norepinephrine release by:","options":[{"t":"Removing the inhibitor of release","correct":true,"why":"α2 is the inhibitory (Gi) autoreceptor; blocking the inhibitor gives a positive effect: more norepinephrine."},{"t":"Blocking the norepinephrine transporter","correct":false,"why":"Blocking the transporter (cocaine) keeps released norepinephrine in the synapse longer; mirtazapine acts on the receptor that decides how much is released."},{"t":"Activating α1 on the nerve terminal","correct":false,"why":"Mirtazapine binds with affinity and no efficacy: it blocks α2, α1, H1 and muscarinic receptors and activates none of them."},{"t":"Inhibiting monoamine oxidase (MAO)","correct":false,"why":"Inhibiting MAO (phenelzine, selegiline) lets more norepinephrine be stored; mirtazapine does not touch the enzyme, it removes the Gi brake on release."}],"teach":"α2 autoreceptors are inhibitory (Gαi) and suppress norepinephrine release. Mirtazapine antagonizes them: inhibiting the inhibitor gives a positive effect, more norepinephrine (and serotonin) release, which helps decrease depression.","quote":"So what do you think is gonna happen to the levels of norepinephrine if I inhibit the alpha 2s? What happens if I inhibit the inhibitor? I get positive effect. So by inhibiting the alpha 2s in the CNS, I'm gonna get less inhibition from the G alphai pathway.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slides 31–32; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-058","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"mirt","skill":"recall","multi":true,"concept":"mirtazapine-receptors","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which receptors does mirtazapine block? Select all that apply.","options":[{"t":"α2","correct":true,"why":"Its main action: presynaptic α2 autoreceptor antagonist."},{"t":"H1","correct":true,"why":"Histamine 1 receptors (sedation)."},{"t":"Muscarinic","correct":true,"why":"Muscarinic receptors (sedation)."},{"t":"α1","correct":true,"why":"It can block α1 as well."},{"t":"β1","correct":false,"why":"No β action was given for mirtazapine."},{"t":"Muscle nicotinic (Nm)","correct":false,"why":"Nm is the neuromuscular receptor; mirtazapine does not cause paralysis."}],"teach":"Mirtazapine is marketed as an α2 antagonist but also blocks histamine H1, muscarinic and α1 receptors. α2 block raises norepinephrine (antidepressant); H1 and muscarinic block cause sedation; α1 block adds its own effects.","quote":"Mirtazapine is our alpha-2 antagonist, but also can block H1s, it can block muscarinics, and it can block alpha-1s. So that's gonna be a fun drug to take, right?","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slides 27, 31–32; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-059","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","level":1,"topic":"L10","sub":"mirt","skill":"apply","concept":"mirtazapine-sedation","tags":["in-class"],"source":"both","stem":"Mirtazapine helps depressed patients who also have insomnia fall asleep. Which receptor blockade accounts for this?","options":[{"t":"Histamine H1 and muscarinic","correct":true,"why":"Blocking histamine and muscarinic receptors in the brain causes sedation."},{"t":"Presynaptic α2 only","correct":false,"why":"α2 block raises norepinephrine, the antidepressant effect, not sleep."},{"t":"α1 and β1","correct":false,"why":"β1 is not one of its receptors, and sedation is tied to H1 and muscarinic block."},{"t":"The norepinephrine transporter","correct":false,"why":"Mirtazapine does not block the transporter."}],"teach":"Mirtazapine is adjunct therapy for major depressive disorder with insomnia. α2 block raises norepinephrine (less depression); H1 and muscarinic block in the brain causes sedation (sleep). The pharmacology explains the use.","quote":"What is the side effect of having a drug that blocks the muscarinic and the histamine receptors? Sedation, right? You're gonna put them to sleep. So this drug not only helps with the depression by increasing repinephrine, but also helps them to fall asleep","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slides 31–32; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-060","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","topic":"L10","sub":"mirt","skill":"tell","concept":"clonidine-vs-mirtazapine","tags":[],"source":"both","stem":"Which of the following statements about the α2 drugs in the central nervous system is CORRECT?","options":[{"t":"Clonidine lowers norepinephrine release","correct":true,"why":"Clonidine activates the inhibitory (Gi) α2 pathway: less norepinephrine release, less sympathetic outflow (↓CNS)."},{"t":"Mirtazapine lowers norepinephrine release","correct":false,"why":"Mirtazapine blocks α2 and enhances release (↑CNS)."},{"t":"Clonidine raises norepinephrine release","correct":false,"why":"Activating α2 suppresses release."},{"t":"Mirtazapine activates α2 receptors","correct":false,"why":"Mirtazapine is the α2 antagonist."}],"teach":"Same receptor, opposite drugs. Clonidine (α2 agonist) activates the Gi pathway: less norepinephrine, less sympathetic (↓CNS). Mirtazapine (α2 antagonist) removes that inhibition: more norepinephrine, more sympathetic (↑CNS).","quote":"So if you think about the CNS, clonidine is going to activate the inhibitory pathway, I get less sympathetic, as mirtazapine is going to enhance the sympathetic.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slide 21; transcript 10/6"},{"id":"L10-061","lecture":"L10","prof":"Gottlieb","tier":"new","level":2,"ladder":"clonidine","ladderName":"Clonidine: α2, an add-on, and stopping it","topic":"L10","sub":"a2","skill":"apply","concept":"alpha2-agonist-plus-stimulant","tags":["ddi"],"source":"both","stem":"A boy with attention deficit hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) needs a high dose of methylphenidate and has lost his appetite. Guanfacine, an α2 agonist similar to clonidine, is added, and the methylphenidate dose is lowered. Which of the following statements is CORRECT?","options":[{"t":"The two drugs have an additive effect on attention","correct":true,"why":"The α2 agonist adds to the stimulant, so attention is kept with less methylphenidate and less loss of appetite."},{"t":"Guanfacine blocks the norepinephrine transporter","correct":false,"why":"Blocking the norepinephrine transporter (NET) is cocaine's mechanism; guanfacine is an α2 agonist."},{"t":"Guanfacine raises the release of norepinephrine","correct":false,"why":"An α2 agonist acts on an inhibitory (Gi) receptor and suppresses norepinephrine release."},{"t":"Guanfacine slows the breakdown of methylphenidate","correct":false,"why":"Guanfacine acts on the α2 receptor, not on how methylphenidate is broken down."},{"t":"Guanfacine is an α1 agonist that raises blood pressure","correct":false,"why":"Guanfacine is an α2 agonist; like clonidine it lowers sympathetic tone."}],"teach":"Methylphenidate is a stimulant: it mainly releases norepinephrine and dopamine, which improves focus but decreases appetite. Clonidine and guanfacine are α2 agonists, and both are used for ADHD. Adding guanfacine to the stimulant gave an additive effect, so the stimulant dose could come down and attention was kept without the loss of appetite: a good drug–drug interaction.","quote":"He was in such a high dose of Ritalin that we had to lower the dose and add a clonidine, so we didn't really add, we add the Intuniv, which is a similar drug to it, so that way they have this additive effect and he could maintain his attention without affecting his appetite.","note":"Taken from his own example on 10/6 (methylphenidate with guanfacine, which he called similar to clonidine). The -idine rule does not fit guanfacine; slide 22 lists it with the α2 agonists.","cite":"PCOL-Adrenergic_PCOL-26s_PTII_pptx.pdf slides 11–12, 22; transcript 10/6"}]};
/* With formulas-in-text: 7 terms of Basic-Pharmacokinetics (src/glossary.js, module
   and lecture left out) and its question ws6-i3-2c (src/q9_worksheets.js line 168),
   at ac2eb73, with the prof, exam, module, lecture, topic, sub, skill, steps, givens
   and check fields left out; its concept block is {h, list} there and is given here
   as the kit's {h, t} sections, one per list item, wording unchanged. Its option
   explanations name terms beside ClT, Cp and a {{frac:}}; its concept block holds
   Cl = {{frac:rate of elimination|Cp}}. */
const FIXPK = {"terms":[{"id":"pharmacokinetics","term":"Pharmacokinetics","group":"Basics and models","def":"Pharmacokinetics is the study of ADME: absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion.","gist":"Study of absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion of a drug over time.","scene":"A course follows how a drug level in the body changes with time as the drug is taken up, spread to tissues, chemically converted and removed. It does not follow the response the drug produces.","hook":"She separates it from pharmacodynamics by the pair of variables: concentration and time here, concentration and response there.","confuse":["pharmacodynamics","biopharmaceutics","clinical-pharmacokinetics"],"quote":"pharmacodynamics, concentration. And Response. OK, pharmacokinetics, concentration and time.","cite":"Introduction.pdf, PDF page 4 (printed slide 4); PDF page 17 (printed slide 15); transcript 08-17","src":"both"},{"id":"elimination","term":"Elimination","group":"Elimination and clearance","def":"Elimination - irreversible loss of drug from the body by all routes. Drug elimination: irreversible removal of drug from the body by all routes of elimination.","gist":"Irreversible loss of drug by every route; covers metabolism and excretion.","scene":"One word covers both the liver converting a drug to a metabolite and the kidney passing it out intact, because both remove the drug from the body for good.","hook":"Her catch-all term: metabolism and excretion both fall under it.","confuse":["excretion","metabolism","disposition","clearance"],"quote":"Elimination refers to the irreversible loss of drugs from the body by all routes, right? So, Metabolism and excretion are both elimination terms, right? Elimination is kind of our catch-all.","cite":"Introduction.pdf, PDF page 5 (printed slide 5); 4---Clearance-and-Elimination.pdf, PDF page 3 (printed slide 3); transcript 08-17","src":"both"},{"id":"bioavailability","term":"Bioavailability (F)","group":"Absorption and oral dosing","def":"Bioavailability - measure of the systemic availability of a drug. {{frac:AUCoral|AUCIV}} = F, where F represents the fraction of oral dose that enters the plasma or the bioavailability factor. AUC is the area under the plasma concentration-time curve after the oral and after the IV dose.","gist":"Fraction of a dose that reaches systemic circulation; taken as 1 for IV doses.","scene":"The exposure measured after an oral dose is divided by the exposure after the same IV dose, giving the share of the oral dose that enters the plasma. For the IV dose that share is taken as 1.","hook":"For IV doses she takes capital F as 1, and a capital F in a stem should make you think oral.","confuse":["fraction-excreted","first-pass-effect","area-under-the-curve","absorption"],"quote":"when you see a capital F, You should think oral.","cite":"Introduction.pdf, PDF page 5 (printed slide 5); PDF page 27 (printed slide 23); transcript 09-14, 09-21","src":"both"},{"id":"plasma","term":"Plasma (Cp = concentration in plasma)","group":"Basics and models","def":"Liquid supernatant obtained after centrifugation of non-clotted whole blood that contains an anticoagulant; the noncellular liquid fraction of whole blood, containing all the proteins including albumin.","gist":"Liquid from spun, unclotted, anticoagulated blood; keeps all proteins including albumin.","scene":"A sample drawn into a tube with heparin is spun in a centrifuge without being allowed to clot. The liquid on top, which still holds albumin, is sent for the drug assay.","hook":"Serum or plasma is what is mostly measured, to keep the drug from interacting with other parts of the sample; a subscript p on C names this fluid.","confuse":["serum","whole-blood"],"quote":"if you see CP, we're looking at concentration of drug in the plasma, CS we're looking at concentration of drug in the serum, or if you just see a C, you can assume whatever.","cite":"Introduction.pdf, PDF page 11 (printed slide 10); PDF page 18 (no printed number); transcript 08-17, 08-19","src":"both"},{"id":"clearance","term":"Clearance (Cl)","group":"Elimination and clearance","def":"Clearance - measure of drug elimination from the body without identifying the mechanism or process; volume of plasma that is cleared of drug per unit time. ClT = kVD = {{frac:D0|AUC}}: total body clearance ClT is the elimination rate constant k times the volume of distribution VD. Equally, it is the dose D0 divided by the area under the concentration-time curve AUC.","gist":"Volume of fluid cleared of drug per unit time; constant for most drugs.","scene":"A drug's k is multiplied by its volume of distribution to give a figure in L/hr that stays the same whether the plasma level is high or low.","hook":"Not on the equation sheet: Cl = k x VD is one to know by heart. Poll: it does not rise as concentration rises.","confuse":["elimination-rate-constant","volume-of-distribution","renal-clearance","creatinine-clearance"],"quote":"Clearance is equal to k times vd.","cite":"2IVBolusAdministration.pdf, slides 8, 10 (PDF page numbering; deck prints no slide numbers); 4---Clearance-and-Elimination.pdf, PDF page 4 (printed slide 4); PDF page 5 (printed slide 5); transcript 08-24","src":"both"},{"id":"total-body-clearance","term":"Total body clearance (ClT)","group":"Elimination and clearance","def":"Total Body Clearance: ClT = ClR + ClH. Also called drug clearance or systemic clearance.","gist":"Sum of renal and hepatic clearance (volume of plasma cleared of drug per unit time).","scene":"A drug's 1.3 L/hr is split into 0.78 L/hr through the kidney and 0.52 L/hr through the liver.","hook":"With no subscript on Cl, assume this one is meant.","confuse":["renal-clearance","hepatic-clearance","creatinine-clearance"],"quote":"if there is no subscript, you should assume that we're talking about total body clearance","cite":"4---Clearance-and-Elimination.pdf, PDF page 8 (printed slide 8); PDF page 12 (printed slide 22); 2IVBolusAdministration.pdf, slide 8 (PDF page numbering; deck prints no slide numbers); transcript 09-14","src":"both"},{"id":"nonlinear-pharmacokinetics","term":"Nonlinear pharmacokinetics","group":"Nonlinear pharmacokinetics","def":"Models in which increased doses or chronic administration cause deviations from the profile observed with single doses of the same drug: the half-life and clearance change, and small changes in dose can give big changes in concentration.","gist":"Parameters change with dose or chronic dosing; small dose changes, big concentration changes.","scene":"Raising a patient's daily dose from 6 to 8 mg/kg/day doubles the steady-state level, from 10 to 20 mg/L.","hook":"Phenytoin is the course's prototype.","confuse":["linear-pharmacokinetics","saturable-pharmacokinetics","zero-order"],"quote":"small changes in dose will lead to big changes in concentration.","cite":"8 - Nonlinear PK.pdf, slides 3, 14, 19 (PDF page numbering); transcript 10-14","src":"both"}],"qs":[{"id":"ws6-i3-2c","tier":"new","concept":"clearance-constant-with-dose","source":"slide","stem":"2. A patient is receiving his antihypertensive medication as 500 mg every 8 hours in the form of an oral capsule. The bioavailability of the drug from this capsule is 80% and the total body clearance in this patient is 2.5 L/hr. c. What is the ClT of the drug in this patient while taking the 1000 mg orally every 8 hours?","options":[{"t":"2.5 L/hr, unchanged","correct":true,"why":"Total body clearance, the volume of plasma cleared of drug per hour, belongs to the patient and the drug, not the dose. Doubling the dose doubles the rate of elimination, ClT × Cp, because Cp doubles; ClT stays 2.5 L/hr."},{"t":"5 L/hr, twice the clearance","why":"This reads clearance as a quantity that scales with the dose, which it does not. What doubles is the rate of elimination, ClT × Cp; the clearance stays 2.5 L/hr."},{"t":"1.25 L/hr, half the clearance","why":"This reads a higher concentration as a slower-clearing patient. Clearance is the elimination rate over the concentration, and both double together, so the ratio stays 2.5 L/hr."},{"t":"2.0 L/hr, the clearance times F","why":"Multiplying clearance by the bioavailability mixes two different quantities. F is the fraction of the oral dose reaching the circulation; clearance is how fast the circulation is cleared. F enters Cavg∞ = {{frac:FD0|ClT τ}}, not ClT."}],"cite":"Multiple-IV-Infusion-and-Multiple-Oral-Solution-3.pdf, problem 2, part c","teach":[{"h":"Clearance does not change with the dose","t":"Clearance is constant because elimination is first order: Cl = {{frac:rate of elimination|Cp}} is the proportionality factor (Module 4 slide \"Clearance\")."},{"t":"It would change only if elimination saturated, which is nonlinear pharmacokinetics."},{"t":"Cavg∞ = {{frac:FD0|ClT τ}} is therefore proportional to the dose: twice the dose, twice the average."}]}]};
